    "cotton": {
      "word": "COTTON",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "A soft, downy substance, resembling fine wool, consisting of the unicellular twisted hairs which grow on the seeds of the cotton plant. Long-staple cotton has a fiber sometimes almost two inches long; short-staple, from two thirds of an inch to an inch and a half.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "The cotton plant. See Cotten plant, below.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "Cloth made of cotton. which commonly needs no explanation; as, cottton bagging; cotton clotch; cotton goods; cotton industry; cotton mill; cotton spinning; cotton tick. Cotton cambric. See Cambric, n., 2. twilled, and with a long plush nap. In England it is called swan's- down cotton, or Canton flannel. by Eli Whitney. family, having delicate capillary bristles surrounding the fruit (seedlike achenia), which elongate at maturity and resemble tufts of cotton. injurious to cotton crops. species, all growing in warm climates, and bearing the cotton of commerce. The common species, originally Asiatic, is G. herbaceum. compressed into smaller bulk for shipment; a press for baling cotton. with a white substance resembling cotton. innumerabilis), which does great damage to the cotton plant. (Dysdercus suturellus), which seriously damages growing cotton by staining it; -- called also redbug. cotton, and the pile is of silk; also, velvet made wholly of cotton. which in the larval state does great damage to the cotton plant by eating the leaves. It also feeds on corn, etc., and hence is often called corn worm, and Southern army worm.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "To rise with a regular nap, as cloth does. [Obs.] It cottons well; it can not choose but bear A pretty nap. Family of Love.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To go on prosperously; to succeed. [Obs.] New, Hephestion, does not this matter cotton as I would Lyly.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To unite; to agree; to make friends; -- usually followed by with. [Colloq.] A quarrel will end in one of you being turned off, in which case it will not be easy to cotton with another. Swift. Didst see, Frank, how the old goldsmith cottoned in with his beggarly companion Sir W. Scott.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To take a liking to; to stick to one as cotton; -- used with to. [Slang]",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        }
      ],
